{{nihongo|'''[[Sho Aoyagi]]'''|青柳 翔|Aoyagi Shō|born March 11, 1979}} is a [[Okaiken|Sanese]] politician and former Chairman of the [[Sanese Communist Party|Sanese Communist Party (SCP)]]. He served as the 55th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]], from July 27, 2016, to August 25, 2021.

==Early life and education==
[[Sho Aoyagi]] was born on March 11, 1979, in a mountainous area of the village of Yamanaka, within the city limits of Ichijoudani, Okaiken. His parents were poor farmers, and the family often struggled to make ends meet. Aoyagi's father cultivated vegetables on a small plot of land they owned. Due to the family's financial situation, Aoyagi was unable to attend school until he was eight years old, and he had to walk over 10 kilometers each way. He would often work for an hour or two before and after school to help his family.

Despite these challenges, Aoyagi was a determined student. After graduating from high school, he attended [[Kawahara University of Foreign Studies]], where he focused on international relations and political theory. It was here that he began to actively engage in student movements and develop his political philosophy, which was heavily influenced by the social and economic disparities he witnessed in his hometown. His studies further cemented his belief in collectivism and the importance of a strong social safety net.

==Music career==
In parallel with his political activism, Aoyagi pursued an interest in music. After attending a concert by the popular pop-rock group "The Workers," he was inspired to audition for a new vocalist position with the band. In a highly competitive "Vocal Battle Audition" held in 2005, Aoyagi was selected as the final winner from thousands of applicants. He officially became the new vocalist of The Workers in 2006, marking what fans called "The Second Era" of the group.

As a member of the band, Aoyagi contributed to three singles and an album, which helped introduce a new sound to their long-time fans. He performed on the group's "The Workers LIVE TOUR 2007" across the nation, further cementing his place in the group. Despite his success in music, Aoyagi's passion for politics remained his primary focus, and he eventually left the band to dedicate himself entirely to his political career.

==Political career==

===Early activism and party rise (1996–2010)===
Aoyagi's political involvement began at age 17 when he joined the student movement affiliated with the Communist Party of Okaiken (CPO). Unlike many of his contemporaries who focused on theoretical Marxism, Aoyagi gained notoriety for "on-the-ground" organizing in the industrial dockyards and agrarian co-ops of the [[Tokami Region]]. He quickly rose through the party's ranks, becoming known for his passionate, unscripted speeches and his ability to connect with the working class through a blend of traditional labor rhetoric and modern populist appeal. 

In 2008, he was appointed as the party's deputy leader by its long-time president, [[Kazuo Shii]], who viewed Aoyagi as the necessary "bridge" to a younger, more disillusioned electorate. During this time, Aoyagi pioneered the "Digital Collectivist Initiative," a policy that utilized early social media to organize wildcat strikes and community-led mutual aid networks, bypassing traditional media gatekeepers.

===Election to the House of Commoners (2011)===
Aoyagi was first elected to the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken]] for the Seto 3rd District on January 20, 2011. His victory was considered a watershed moment for the CPO, as he managed to flip a seat that had been held by the conservative establishment for over three decades. His campaign focused heavily on "The Forgotten Okaiken," targeting the economic stagnation in the shipbuilding and heavy manufacturing sectors. 

[[File:Aoyagi Campaign.png|thumb|right|250px|Aoyagi speaking at a rally in the Seto 3rd District shortly before his historic 2011 election victory.]]

In his first term, he introduced the '''Rural Infrastructure Act''', which sought to redirect urban development funds toward failing rural healthcare clinics. Though the bill was initially defeated, it earned Aoyagi cross-party respect and established him as a legislator who looked beyond ideological purity.

===Leadership and the Imperial Referendum===
As deputy leader, Aoyagi was a vocal critic of the referendum to restore the symbolic powers of the [[Honda clan|Imperial Family of Okaiken]], a policy advocated by former Prime Minister [[Tadahisa Johei]]. Aoyagi argued that the restoration was a "revanchist distraction" designed to mask the failures of neoliberal economic policies. His performance during the televised "Great Debates" of 2015 solidified his status as a national figure, as he famously debated the Crown Prince on the floor of the Diet—a move that was considered scandalous by traditionalists but lauded by the SCP's base.

In a surprising turn of events in early 2016, there were widespread rumors that the party's third-in-command, [[Sachiro Ishimoto]], would succeed Shii. However, following a series of closed-door meetings at the party's headquarters in [[Okami]], Ishimoto stepped aside at the last minute, allowing Aoyagi to become the leader of the Communist Party on May 8, 2016.

===The "United Left" strategy===
Following his ascension to leadership, Aoyagi shifted the SCP's strategy away from isolationism. He was a key architect in the informal "Tactical Non-Aggression Pact" with other left-leaning independents, including a young [[Aono Michifumi]]. This strategy was designed to consolidate the anti-conservative vote in key districts. 

His "New Left" policies—which combined environmentalism with industrial nationalization—became the blueprint for the SCP's 2016 general election platform. He argued that the state should not just provide a safety net, but should actively own the "commanding heights" of the economy to prevent the corporate corruption seen in the [[Paraña Bank Scandal]]. This shift in rhetoric is widely credited with bringing the SCP from a perennial third party to the plurality winner in 2016.

==Prime Minister (2016–2021)==
After the [[Sanese Communist Party|SCP]] won a historic plurality in the [[Okaiken general election, 2016|2016 general election]] amid the LDP's "Paraña Bank Scandal," Aoyagi formed a "left-wing" coalition government. He was sworn in as the 55th Prime Minister of Okaiken on July 27, 2016. His administration marked a historic shift, as it was the first time in Okaiken's history that a government was led by the SCP.

Aoyagi's premiership was characterized by a series of ambitious reforms aimed at re-balancing the nation's economic structure. A cornerstone of his platform was the Universal Basic Services (UBS) initiative, which aimed to provide all citizens with free access to essential services such as public transportation, utilities, and internet access, funded through a new progressive wealth tax. His administration also passed the Okaiken Workers' Collective Act, which mandated worker representation on the boards of all major private corporations, introduced a nationwide living wage, and strengthened the right to unionize.

Additionally, Aoyagi's government moved to nationalize the energy and telecommunications sectors, arguing that these vital services should be managed for the public good. In his most contentious move, the administration targeted the powerful agricultural cooperative system (OA), seeking to break up large land holdings and redistribute them to younger farmers. This policy met fierce resistance from the OA Group, which organized a powerful opposition campaign. To fund these expansive social programs, Aoyagi's government increased the national corporate tax rate from 37% to 43%.

While these reforms were popular with his base, they were met with fierce resistance from the business community and the opposition [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|PPO]]. The latter part of his tenure was marked by political gridlock, protests, and severe inflation in 2020. Aoyagi's premiership was critically wounded by a major gaffe in early 2021, when he inadvertently admitted during a Diet questioning to increasing the national defense budget. This move, intended to be a "pragmatic" step, was seen as a profound betrayal by his party's pacifist base and his coalition partners, who had been promised cuts to military spending.

The combination of high inflation, backlash from the business and agricultural sectors, and the defense spending gaffe led to a significant defeat for the Communist Party in the [[Okaiken general election, 2021|2021 parliamentary election]], where the SCP lost 48 seats. Aoyagi resigned from the premiership on August 25, 2021, and was succeeded by [[Honda Shigetsuma]].

==Post-Premiership==
Following the SCP's disastrous performance in the 2021 election, Aoyagi took responsibility for the defeat and announced his resignation as Chairman of the Sanese Communist Party. On November 1, 2021, he was formally replaced by the party's hard-line faction leader, [[Shintaro Hamabe]].

Aoyagi retained his seat in the Seto 3rd District in the 2021 election and again in the [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025 general election]], surviving the "Hamabe-led" collapse of the party that saw its seat count halved and Hamabe himself lose his seat. Aoyagi currently serves as a regular member of the House of Commoners. He is widely seen as the leader of the party's "pragmatic" or "Etruscan" wing, often in quiet opposition to the hard-line faction that took control after his resignation.

==Personal life==
Aoyagi is married to Tsutsui Keiko, whom he wed in 2007. The couple has two children: Shintaro and Tsuneko.

